    "headline": "Saru's ultimate derivation is possibly borrowed from Ainu サロ (saro, “monkey”).",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "From Old Japanese. Appears in the Man'yōshū, completed some time after 759 CE."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Ultimate derivation possibly borrowed from Ainu サロ (saro, “monkey”, from サㇻ (sar, “a tail”) + オ (o, “to bear, to wear, to carry”))."
      },
      {
        "quote": "The kanji is from Chinese 猿 (yuán, “ape”). Compare Japanese 猪 (inoshishi, “boar”) from Chinese 猪 (zhū, “pig”) and Japanese 豚 (buta, “pig”) from Chinese 豚 (tún, “suckling pig”)."
      }
    ],
    "otherReadings": [
      {
        "readings": ["まし"],
        "quotes": [
          "From Old Japanese. Appears in the Man'yōshū, completed some time after 759 CE, used phonetically to spell the sound /masi/.",
          "Ultimate derivation unknown."
        ]
      },
      {
        "readings": ["ましら"],
        "quotes": [
          "Derived from earlier mashi form above. Found in texts from the early 1900s, possibly earlier. Appears to be mashi + the pluralizing and genericizing suffix ら (ra)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "readings": ["えん"],
        "quotes": [
          "From Middle Chinese 猿 (MC hjwon).",
